  - [1m[38;5;153mGET /notifications[39m is always scoped to the session user; there is no [38;5;153muser_id[39m parameter.[0m Notification lists are the
    classic IDOR target, and the way you don't have that bug is by making it unrepresentable in the API rather than by
    checking authorization carefully.

  [1mFailure handling — the part that matters most, since a notification system's job is to not lose things:[0m

  ┌───────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │        Failure        │                                        Behavior                                         │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Worker crashes        │ Event stays [38;5;153mprocessed_at IS NULL[39m, gets reclaimed next tick. Requires step 3 to be       │
  │ mid-event             │ idempotent — enforced by a [38;5;153mUNIQUE(event_id, recipient_id)[39m index on [38;5;153mnotification_events[39m, │
  │                       │  so a replayed event can't double-notify.                                               │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ One event throws      │ [38;5;153mattempts++[39m with backoff; after 5, mark it failed and move on. One poison event must     │
  │ repeatedly            │ never stall the queue behind it.                                                        │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Adapter fails (email  │ [38;5;153mdeliveries.state = 'failed'[39m, retried with backoff. Other channels for the same          │
  │ down)                 │ notification are unaffected.                                                            │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Notification read     │ Pending non-in-app deliveries are skipped. You shouldn't get an email about something   │
  │ before delivery fires │ you already saw.                                                                        │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Task or user deleted  │ Cascade-delete their notifications. A notification pointing at a task that no longer    │
  │                       │ exists has nothing useful to say.                                                       │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Worker not running    │ Task mutations still succeed; events accumulate and drain when it restarts. Degraded,   │
  │                       │ not broken — this is the main payoff of the outbox.                                     │
  └───────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  One constraint to record for later: in the slice every user can see every task, so interest resolution can't leak
  anything. When permissions arrive, [38;5;153minterestedUsers[39m must intersect with [3mcan-view[0m — otherwise a notification's title
  text becomes a disclosure channel for tasks the recipient isn't allowed to see.

